A||||13||dman||[Q] Vehicle Speed Sensor||||5||
Z||000000||dman||05-08-2000||05:41 PM||||Hello all, I hope that someone here can help me. One of my fieros (its my parts car) has some wiring on the tranny that is disconnected. I'm pretty sure it is the speed sensor. The car is an 85 GT (v6 with 4speed muncie trannie). On top and towards the rear is a connector that has two wires coming out of it. The wires are broken off and I have no clue to where they connect to. There is a small loom going by the drivers side shock tower that also has some wires disconnected but I'm pretty sure it connects to the blower that is mounted inside the trunk area (that blower only seems to exists on 85-86 v6 cars). I don't see any other wires or looms that even go near the speed sensor. Can someone take a look under their hood and help me please? I think that this may be part of the reason the car runs so rough. The ECM complains about a faulty speed sensor and I suspect that not having an operational one that the ECM is running in one of those reduced capacity (failsafe?) modes.<P>Thanks in advance everyone!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||05-08-2000||06:34 PM||||It sounds like the VSS leads got caught in the axle and torn out. Someone with another 85 will have to help you with the wiring. You can get replacement wiring from <A HREF="http://www.kickhill.com" TARGET=_blank>Kickhill</A><P>Correct, the VSS feeds the speedometer which divides the signal by 2 and feeds it to the ECM and Cruise unit if equiped.<P>The ECM will run in a fail mode with out the VSS. DO NOT reset the ECM until you fix the VSS. You could end up with it running even worse.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dman||05-08-2000||07:08 PM||||Thanks theOgre! I suspected the ECM would probably make the car run funny (or run funnier). I definitely have an exhaust leak as well but I want to fix that VSS wiring to eliminate as many unknowns as possible.<P>So let me ask again -- can someone with a 4speed muncie please check their wiring and tell me what my VSS sensor should be hooked up to? Thanks again!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||dman||05-09-2000||02:35 PM||||Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||05-09-2000||05:41 PM||||If you're asking which wires it should be connected to, I believe that I remember the wires being one purple and one yellow.||||reg||1
Z||000005||dman||05-09-2000||06:12 PM||||Thanks batboy for replying. Unfortunately that doesn't really answer my question but at least now I know what colors the wires are (they were to dirty and aged to make out the color).<P>On the top and towards the rear of my transmission is the vehicle speed sensor. There is a two wire connector going to it. One wire is gone, and the other is only two inches long. They somehow got disconnected from the wiring harness.<P>My question is this: where is the wiring harness that this connector should be attached to?<P>I want to reattach the connector but I don't know where it should go.<P>Would a picture help?<P>Thanks again batboy.<P>BTW, you haven't provided us any batmobile updates in a while. Still too busy with the other project?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||05-09-2000||10:28 PM||||On my 85 GT (which is a 4 speed), if I remember right, the two wires go from the speed sensor slightly down and directly to the right (as you're standing at the rear of the car facing towards the front). They're in a plastic corrugated conduit which is clipped onto the lower part of the engine or cradle (?). Seems like it goes a foot or two and joins into a larger conduit. I'll go look after work tomorrow while there's still light and see if I can stick my digital camera down in there for a pic. I know one wire is purple, not sure if they both were purple or if the other one was yellow.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 05-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||05-09-2000||10:42 PM||||Oh yeah, nothing recent to report on the Batmobile. Gotta run it into the alignment shop soon. That's the only thing stopping it from driving right now. I'm hoping to get some more done in the next couple of weeks.<P>I finally got my rule book in the mail from Heartland Park Dragstrip. I was told they were a stickler for relocated batteries and sure enough, they have some special rules. I have to make a couple minor modifications to my setup now.<P>No worries about breaking into the 11's with the engine that's currently in it, but with my new one I might...if so, they require a rollbar. I don't want a rollbar in my Fiero...or do I? Sort of loses the "sleeper" effect, huh? Hmmm...||||reg||1
Z||000008||dman||05-09-2000||10:53 PM||||Wow, the batmobile sounds like its close to being finished! <P>And thanks again batboy. I'm hoping to fix this thing tonite. At least now I have a clue where else to look.<P>Here's a pic I took this morning:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=840623&a=6111033&p=20933645&Sequence=0"> <P>The white arrow shows the VSS. Note the single (dirty) lead coming from it. Though the picture doesn't show it, it isn't hooked up to anything.<P>The red arrow shows a black conduit with a few more broken wires. Those however seem to go to the blower that is mounted inside the trunk as well as connecting to that door (actually decklid) switch.<P>I'm hoping that someone can look under their hood and tell me which wire loom (conduit, harness whatever) the VSS wires should go to.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||05-10-2000||12:30 AM||||I took pity on you and went out to look. My mini-mag flashlight had half dead batteries, but I saw that I remembered correctly. Forget the harness by the red arrow. The one you want goes off to the lower right and clips to the tranny and engine. It's probably very greasy, dirty and hard to see. It's tucked down there practically out of sight. In fact, you may have to jack it up and crawl under it to find the right harness.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||05-10-2000||12:37 AM||||BTW, I didn't mean to give you the impression the Batmobile was almost "done". Stop! Don't spread that untrue gossip! I merely meant that it was almost roadworthy again. It's at least another year from being what I would consider finished. Besides, are projects ever really "done"? Just trying to build it with NHRA rules in mind, just in case...||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||05-11-2000||02:01 PM||||I believe the VSS wires are in the harness that runs along the rear side of the engine below the alternator.  There is a clip bolted to the block below the rear valve cover that grabs this harness.  I believe the alternator wires are also in this harness.<P>If you don't have an answer by this evening I will scan the diagram from my 85 service manual and upload it via PIP.  That's assuming AOL is back on line tonight.  Last night they were down.||||reg||1
Z||000012||dman||05-11-2000||10:06 PM||||Thank you both for your responses! Responses like that make this forum the best, most up to date source of Fiero information.<P>I have reconnected my VSS. Neither Pep-Boys or Autozone carry a direct replacement connector, but they did have connectors with wires for most everything else (MAP, IAC etc). Pep boys did have a connector shell, inner rubber boots, and the male terminals that comprise the VSS connector --- but they were all in their own packages. Each package cost about $2.30. So it was like 7 bucks for this little connector.<P>Anyways, the speedometer now works. Yippee!!! But the ECM still complains about a speed sensor error (actually my scantool says it, but the SES light blinks the corresponding code).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||05-11-2000||10:26 PM||||Maybe you haven't reset the ECM? Once it stores a trouble code, it's my understanding that it stays stored until you reset it. Take the negative battery cable off for a minute or two and then put it back on. It might take a few miles of driving for your car to reprogram itself, but it'll smooth back out again.||||reg||1
